    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes and the test suite is green. The audit log confirms the new behaviour end-to-end: `membershipNumber=NSW-16E6609E-M27` for a `registrationDate=2026-09-11` (September \u2192 fiscal year FY27, since the fiscal year starting 1 July 2026 ends in 2027).\n\n## Summary\n\nMoved all date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts 1 July, named by the calendar year it ends in, formatted `FY<YY>`), driven off the business-day-adjusted registration date already stored by `BuildOwner`.\n\n**New helper**\n- `rest/function/common/FiscalYear.java` \u2014 `of(LocalDate)` returns the fiscal year (`month >= July ? year+1 : year`); `label(LocalDate)` returns `FY<YY>`.\n\n**Behaviour changes**\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` field mapping; the `membershipNumber` year segment now uses `FiscalYear.of(...)` instead of the raw calendar year.\n- `rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java` \u2014 the duplicate membership-number logic in the audit line now uses the fiscal year too, keeping it in sync.\n- `rest/function/common/MembershipLevels.java` \u2014 tenure points are now awarded on **elapsed fiscal years** (`FiscalYear.of(now) - FiscalYear.of(registrationDate) > 1`) rather than a 365-day window; replaced `TENURE_DAYS_FOR_POINTS = 365` with `TENURE_FISCAL_YEARS_FOR_POINTS = 1` and dropped the `ChronoUnit` usage.\n\n**Contract**\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `fiscalYear` property to the `Owner` schema (so `OwnerDto` is generated with it), and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to the fiscal-year wording.\n\nOne design decision worth flagging: I used the standard Australian convention where the fiscal year is named by the year it **ends** (1 July 2026\u201330 June 2027 \u2192 `FY27`), which fits the app's AU locale data. The tenure threshold was mapped from \"more than 365 days\" (\u2248 one year) to \"more than one elapsed fiscal year.\" The acceptance test only pins the `FY\\d{2}` format, so if you intended the start-year convention instead, that's a one-line change in `FiscalYear.of`.",
